Give empty arrays a real backing pointer.

array_new passed a zero byte request to m_malloc for an empty bytearray or array.array, producing
items=NULL. Generic array, buffer, string-printing and Unicode paths nevertheless form items + 0.
That is undefined pointer arithmetic in C, and on Capstone it lowers to cincoffset with a
non-capability NULL base.

Allocate one spare element for an empty array and record it in the existing free count. This keeps
the allocation metadata truthful, preserves observable length and contents, and lets later append
or extension paths reuse the reserve normally.

A focused EXTRA+MPZ run changes bytearray_add.py, bytearray_byte_operations.py,
bytearray_decode.py and bytearray_slice_assign.py from capability faults to PASS; all nine direct
tests between them retain their PASS status and result words.

diff --git i/py/objarray.c w/py/objarray.c
index 75bc671c1..02314d9e3 100644
--- i/py/objarray.c
+++ w/py/objarray.c
@@ -105,9 +105,12 @@ static mp_obj_array_t *array_new(char typecode, size_t n) {
     o->base.type = &mp_type_array;
     #endif
     o->typecode = typecode;
-    o->free = 0;
+    // Keep a real pointer even for an empty array. Generic array and buffer
+    // operations may form items + 0, which is undefined when items is NULL
+    // and cannot be represented as a capability-derived pointer.
+    o->free = n == 0;
     o->len = n;
-    o->items = m_new(byte, typecode_size * o->len);
+    o->items = m_new(byte, typecode_size * (o->len + o->free));
     return o;
 }
 #endif
